// Contract probe (E39): `@prove(no_checks)` on `Grid.get` and `Grid.set` can't be proven in
// either build, so the library doesn't carry it. (`@prove(no_alloc)` holds for `Grid[u8]` but
// not for a T that owns heap memory, so the library doesn't carry that either; `tests/grid.kes`
// proves it for `Grid[u8]`, E72.) This test adds `no_checks` to the scratch copy of `grid.kes`
// only and wants the build to refuse it, naming both methods. When a compiler change makes it
// pass, this test fails: move the contract into the library.
//
// What stops it (from `kestrel check --report`): the cell's list index,
// `(y * sz + z) * sx + x`, is any `int` to the compiler (the coordinates are the caller's),
// so the read and the write keep their bounds check (and, in the checked build, the index
// maths its overflow checks). By design: `get` and `set` don't check that the cell is inside
// (`inside` is for that), so only the list's own check stops a bad index.
use { grid from grid }

main() {
    var g = grid[u8](4, 3, 2, u8(0))
    g.set(1, 2, 1, u8(7))
    print(g.get(1, 2, 1))
}
// replace: grid.kes     pure get(self, x: int, y: int, z: int) -> T { =>     @prove(no_checks) pure get(self, x: int, y: int, z: int) -> T {
// replace: grid.kes     pure set(var self, x: int, y: int, z: int, v: T) { =>     @prove(no_checks) pure set(var self, x: int, y: int, z: int, v: T) {
// expect-exit: 1
// Each error names its method ("`Grid[u8].get` (made by `Grid[u8]` on <line>) can't be proven
// `no_checks`: ..."); the line in this file is left out so editing the header doesn't break it.
// expect-stderr: `Grid[u8].get` (made by `Grid[u8]` on
// expect-stderr: `Grid[u8].set` (made by `Grid[u8]` on
// expect-stderr: can't be proven `no_checks`
// release
